      "id": "4erTHCyRsV8F",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4erTHCyRsV8F"
      },
      "source": [
        "The qualitative results show that both models can answer accurately when the relevant evidence is retrieved, since all best examples for both Qwen and Gemma have recall@k = 1.0 and precision@k = 0.2. In the best cases, Qwen reaches slightly higher peak performance, including one exact match with token_f1 = 1.00 and several other very strong answers above 0.90, suggesting that it can closely reproduce the ground-truth wording when the retrieved context is clear and specific. Gemma’s best examples are also strong, though generally with slightly lower top scores, showing that it can still generate correct and well-aligned answers but tends to paraphrase more rather than match the reference as closely as Qwen in its strongest cases.\n",
        "\n",
        "The worst examples, however, highlight a clearer difference in consistency. Qwen’s lowest-scoring cases are much weaker, including answers such as “Not specified in the document” even when the correct page was retrieved, which suggests that its main problem is not retrieval failure but generation failure or inability to extract the needed detail from the retrieved text. In contrast, Gemma’s worst cases are generally incomplete or loosely phrased rather than completely incorrect, and its lowest scores are not as extreme overall. This suggests that while Qwen may achieve better exact matches in some individual questions, Gemma is more stable across the dataset, producing fewer severe failures and more consistently usable answers."
      ]
    },
