        "id": "V7QCvCtNfAzt"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **Section 9. Insights and Conclusions**"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "4DJXVGRZ8S-O",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4DJXVGRZ8S-O"
      },
      "source": [
        "The study shows that the proposed NBCP-based RAG chatbot is effective in retrieving relevant provisions from the National Building Code of the Philippines, as both models achieved Recall@k = 1.0 and Precision@k = 0.2 across 21 evaluation questions. This indicates that the retrieval pipeline consistently returned at least one relevant chunk for every query, although the retrieved context still contained some irrelevant text. The main difference therefore came from the generation stage rather than the retrieval stage. Among the two models, Gemma performed better overall, achieving higher Token F1 (0.6078 vs. 0.5637), higher token recall (0.5963 vs. 0.5354), and stronger numeric recall and unit recall (both 0.9048) than Qwen, which is especially important for regulation-based question answering where exact measurements, values, and units must be preserved accurately.\n",
        "\n",
        "Through the development of the system, several practical insights were also gained regarding the design of LLM-based regulatory chatbots. One key observation is that prompt design plays a significant role in the final performance of the system. During experimentation, modifying the prompt instructions—such as enforcing single-sentence answers, requiring strict grounding in the retrieved context, or instructing the model to return “Not specified in the document” when evidence is absent—resulted in noticeable changes in evaluation metrics such as Token F1, precision, and recall. This indicates that prompt engineering is not only a usability feature but also a critical factor affecting measurable model performance in RAG-based systems.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the findings suggest that the major bottleneck of the system is not evidence retrieval, but the model’s ability to extract and present the correct answer from the retrieved context. Gemma was more stable across questions and recorded fewer generation failures, while Qwen, although slightly better in token precision and capable of stronger individual best-case answers, was less consistent overall. These results imply that for compliance-oriented chatbot applications, model selection should prioritize completeness, consistency, and preservation of technical details rather than only concise phrasing. Future improvements may therefore focus on stronger prompt constraints, reranking or compressing retrieved chunks, and post-generation verification of numbers and units to further improve reliability in NBCP question answering."
      ]
    },
